Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox 2026.9.2 --- Current User-Visible Changes

This patch release requires Emacs 31 or later and Omnivox 1.7.0 or newer.  The
guided WSL2 installer pins the matching verified Omnivox 1.7.0 Windows x64 or
ARM64 archive.

Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.9.1][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.9.1]].

** First-run engine selection and WSL setup

~C-e d e~ now works before a machine routing profile has been saved.  It uses
the live Omnivox policy as a transient session baseline and writes no file;
~C-u C-e d e~ explicitly saves the baseline with the selected engine order.

The WSL2 installation guide now gives a concrete Linux-filesystem checkout
location, an update procedure that leaves the current speaking session running
until its replacement is ready, manual rebuild steps, separate Emacsvox and
Omnivox version checks, and immediate controls for slowing general speech and
echoed characters.  The guided installer now fully rebuilds byte-code so an
update cannot retain generated Lisp from the previous source revision.

** Omnivox 1.7.0

The supported speech-server baseline is now Omnivox 1.7.0.  The guided WSL2
installer pins the published generic Windows archives and their checksums, and
the clean source-built Windows runtime pins the matching source commit and
Piper companion.  This aligns Emacsvox's portable chorus styles and native
helper integrations with Omnivox's 1.7.0 protocol and engine support.

The new ~M-x emacsvox-omnivox-manage-components~ command, also available as
~I~ from Aural Home, presents a spoken list of built-in, downloadable, and
user-runtime speech engines.  On WSL2 it can checksum-verify, stage, validate,
and install the version-matched Flite, RuTTS, Piper, or experimental
TGSpeechBox companions per user.  It never downloads proprietary Eloquence,
DECtalk, or RHVoice runtimes or chooses a Piper voice model.

** Org link transitions

Opening an Org link into another buffer now plays one opening cue while leaving
the destination integration responsible for speaking its content.

** Agent Shell response speech

At the default response speech level, one completed turn now produces one
bounded automatic presentation instead of queueing every rendered answer
fragment separately.  Automatic response speech shares navigation's
replacement domain, so ordinary Up and Down movement promptly interrupts it.
Full speech level retains its detailed per-block ordering, and explicit
~C-c r~ reading still speaks the complete response.

** Notmuch search navigation

Speaking each search-result row no longer copies the complete Omnivox engine
and voice inventory.  This substantially reduces allocation while navigating
large Notmuch result buffers and avoids the associated long garbage-collection
pauses.  Foreground search completion now keeps its view summary and selected
message fields in separate semantic objects, avoiding an invalid presentation
rule error when the first result is announced.

Quitting a Notmuch view with ~q~ now interrupts message or search-result speech
before playing the close cue.

** Vertico history

Recalling minibuffer history while Vertico is active now speaks the recalled
entry once instead of repeating it during Vertico's following display update.

** Terminal completion

EAT no longer mistakes unchanged terminal history above the prompt for
completion candidates when an inline Bash completion redraw arrives in
multiple chunks.

** Voice tuning

Opening Voice Workbench without a saved routing profile now reuses its initial
voice-inventory snapshot instead of copying the complete inventory twice.

The voice tuner now names neutral post-synthesis values such as /unchanged/ and
/disabled/.  Gain, pan, and filter endpoints are translated to their exact
Omnivox wire values instead of treating every dimension as the same linear
zero-to-nine scale.

Portable voice styles can now request Omnivox chorus.  The default voice
palette also includes a ~telephone~ logical voice that combines the existing
high-pass and low-pass filters for a narrow-band sound without changing speech
duration or markers.

Omnivox average-pitch differences are gentler by default.  Customize
=omnivox-average-pitch-contrast= to =1.0= for the historical curve, =0= for
neutral average pitch, or a value through =2.0= for stronger contrast.

** Image and PDF OCR

The Emacsvox OCR front end now uses PaddleOCR PP-StructureV3 by default.  It
accepts images and PDFs, preserves reading order, emits table-aware Markdown,
and keeps engine diagnostics separate from recognized text.  The user manual
includes the tested x86-64 WSL2 installation for =paddleocr[all]=.  Language,
device, table, formula, orientation, unwarping, acceleration, and Python
selection are available as typed Emacs customization options.
